        summary = "The case Worldwide eradication of smallpox shows a mandate from the World Health Assembly in 1966 and validation of the outcome by a body of experts from 19 countries in Geneva in December 1979. The dates differ by milestone; vaccines alone did not produce the result, which relied on decades of work, diplomacy and technical innovation.",
        details = "Inferred from its principle that users monitor and enforce their own rules, so when a community does not monitor vaccination coverage or enforce sanitation rules, free-riders leave gaps that let disease return to all, and rules made locally may not connect to the wider bodies that control outbreaks. There is no documented case in the pool. Source: Ostrom, Governing the Commons",
    ),

    entry(
        databaseId = 219,
        kind = Kind.COMPROMISE,
        section = "Compromise",
        title = "Compromise: public health",
        originalTitle = "Compromise proposal",
        author = "This catalogue, as a proposal (not an established model)",
        year = 2026,
        summary = "A mixed public system sets national standards for vaccination, screening and sanitation, financed from general taxation. Local boards and several licensed suppliers deliver the work under rules agreed by the bodies that share the goal, checked each year by an independent expert panel.",
        details = "Keeps: shared rules checked by an expert panel (Commons governance; Worldwide eradication of smallpox); local delivery under a national standard (Fabian socialism; Chadwick's sanitary inquiry and the Public Health Act of 1848); several licensed suppliers, figures unconfirmed (Social market economy; The Salk polio vaccine trial and its announcement, 1954 to 1955); tax-funded care, free at the point of use (Nordic social democracy). Blocks: favours by kin or rank, guarded by a statutory entitlement; campaigns stopped in each boom or slump, guarded by ring-fenced funding; targets met on paper, guarded by independent audits. Test: coverage, notified cases, water and sewerage access and cost per person, measured yearly in every district until the programme ends. Source: Ohio State University, The WHO and the eradication of smallpox; Victorian Web, Edwin Chadwick and the sanitary report; University of Michigan School of Public Health, 1955 polio vaccine trial announcement; Sahlins, Stone Age Economics; Polanyi, The Great Transformation; Keynes, The General Theory; Kornai, The Socialist System; Esping-Andersen, The Three Worlds of Welfare Capitalism.",
    ),
